---
description: This rule explains how to create new .mdc project rule files for the Cursor agent.
globs: **/.cursor/rules/**/*.mdc
alwaysApply: false
---
# Cursor Rules Location

Enforces the location, naming conventions, and structure for all other cursor rules files.

1. Always place rule files in PROJECT_ROOT/.cursor/rules/:
    ```
    .cursor/rules/
    ├── global/
    │   ├── transpilation/
    │   │   ├── presenters.mdc
    │   │   ├── services.mdc
    │   │   └── ...
    │   ├── refactoring/
    │   │   └── file-reorganization.mdc
    │   └── cursor/
    │       └── rule-creation.mdc
    ├── anemone_sdk/
    │   └── service-instructions.mdc
    ├── playtomic_ui
    │   └── figma-components.mdc
    └── modules/
        ├── payments/
        |   └── stripe-integration.mdc
        └── ...

    ```


2. Follow the naming convention:
    - Use kebab-case for filenames (e.g., my-rule-name.mdc)
    - Always use .mdc extension
    - Make names descriptive of the rule's purpose

3. Directory organization:
    - `global/`: Project-wide rules and guidelines
    - `modules/domain/`: Business domain or feature-specific rules
    - `terraform/`: Rules for terraform code
    - ...

4. Never place rule files:
    - In the project root
    - In subdirectories outside .cursor/rules
    - In any other location

5. Cursor rules have the following structure:

```
---
description: Short description of the rule's purpose
globs: optional/path/pattern/**/*
alwaysApply: false|true
---

# Rule Title (clear and concise)

Main content explaining the rule for humans using markdown formatting.
- Include context and/or motivation that helps to understand the "when" and the "why".
- Include short, focused examples.

Instructions for IA
- Use imperative verbs
- Allow content duplication if it makes AI more likely to follow the instructions correctly.
```

# Core Principles
- Keep rules concise (under 500 lines)
- Focus on a single, well-defined concern
- Provide concrete examples and references
- Avoid verbose explanations or redundant context

6. Optionally reference other related rules by using @filename.mdc syntax
